duction, then we break? Actually if n == len(current) and no reduction, we will loop again with same n? The algorithm says \"otherwise double it up to current size\". So if n already equals current size, we cannot increase further. In classic ddmin, when n == len(current), the next step with n set to min(2*n, len(current)) will still be len(current), leading to infinite loop if no reduction. Usually, after testing all subsets (each of size 1) and complements, if no reduction, then the configuration is 1-minimal and we stop. But careful: In ddmin, when n equals len(current), the subsets are each of size 1. The complements of size len-1 are also tested. If none FAIL, then the current set is 1-minimal. So we should break after that iteration. But our loop condition: while True, and break when m==1 or when n reaches len(current) and no reduction? Let's implement: after testing subsets and complements without reduction, if n == len(current): break (since we have tried all possible single deletions). But we also need to consider that when n == len(current), we still need to run the loop to test subsets (size1) and complements. So we can change loop: while len(current) > 1: ... at end of loop, if no reduction and n == len(current): break. else set n = min(2*n, len(current)). But careful: when we first enter loop with n=2 and len(current)>1, we do partitioning. If len(current)=3, m=2, subsets sizes 2 and 1. Then if no reduction, we will set n=min(4,3)=3, then next iteration m=3, subsets size 1 each. That's fine. So we can have while True, break when m==1 (i.e., len(current)==1) but that's not minimal, because we might have len(current)=1 but that's already minimal. The algorithm ends when the current set cannot be reduced further. A common condition: when no reduction occurs and n >= len(current), the configuration is 1-minimal. So we can break when n >= len(current) and no reduction. But we need to ensure that when n > len(current), we cap at len(current). So we can set n = min(2*n, len(current)) before next iteration. If after doubling, n == len(current) and we had no reduction, we need to do one more iteration with subsets of size 1. Actually we should perform the loop for each n, and after the loop if no reduction, we increase n. If after increasing, n equals len(current), we want to run one more loop to test singletons and complements. So break condition after the loop's no-reduction block: if n == len(current): break (because next iteration would be same). But we also have the case when we entered loop with n >= len(current) from start (e.g., len(current)=2, n=2). Then m=2, subsets size1. Test each subset (size1). If none FAIL, test complements (size1). If none FAIL, then we would break because n==len(current). That's correct: minimal set of size2? Actually if len(current)=2 and neither single item nor the other single item (complement) fails, then the 2-item set is minimal because removing either item causes PASS. So break.\n\nSo algorithm:\n- current = full list\n- n = 2\n- tests = 1 (initial test)\n- while True:\n   if len(current) == 1: break\n   m = min(n, len(current))\n   partition into m subsets as evenly as possible (ensuring order)\n   reduced = False\n   for subset in subsets:\n        tests += 1\n        if test(subset) == \"FAIL\":\n            current = subset\n            n = 2\n            reduced = True\n            break\n   if reduced: continue\n   for subset in subsets:\n        complement = [x for x in current if x not in subset]  # retains order\n        tests += 1\n        if test(complement) == \"FAIL\":\n            current = complement\n            n = 2\n            reduced = True\n            break\n   if reduced: continue\n   if n == len(current): break\n   n = min(2*n, len(current))\n\nReturn: {\"minimal_failure\": current, \"outcome\": \"FAIL\", \"tests\": tests}\n\nNow we need to write the Python function. We'll include the test oracle inside solve.\n\nWe must adhere to the output contract: return